@@@@ why-hash-pointer-repair-cascade
situation: why
topic: Hash pointers and the chain of blocks
options: {"why":true}
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"correct","askTypes":["predict","explain","short"],"must":["block 2"],"askMustNot":["block 3","block 6'?s (own )?hash","tip"]}
noverify: a conceptual Why-mode exchange; nothing is computed
@@ tutor
Start from two blocks. Block 2 contains, among its fields, the hash of the whole of block 1.

<ask type="predict">Someone changes a transaction inside block 1 and leaves block 2 untouched. What does a node that re-checks the chain notice, and where?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
It hashes block 1 and gets a different hash from the one written in block 2, so the mismatch shows up at block 2's stored pointer.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"a different hash from the one written in block 2" is the detection: block 1 no longer matches the value block 2 was built on, so the break shows at block 2's pointer.</grade>

Now let the forger repair that. They overwrite the pointer in block 2 with the new hash of block 1, so that link checks out again. But the pointer is one of block 2's fields, so block 2 is no longer the block it was.

<ask type="predict">The chain has blocks 1 to 6. After that repair inside block 2, which stored value in the chain no longer matches, and why does fixing that one not finish the forger's work?</ask>

@@@@ why-contract-random-number-vote
situation: why
topic: Smart contracts and deterministic execution
options: {"why":true}
voice: overconfident
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"wrong","names":["vote|majority","different (number|winner|result)|own (number|winner)"],"must":["1,000","order"],"askTypes":["predict","short","explain"],"askMustNot":["\\b20\\b","twenty"]}
@@ tutor
Thousands of nodes each execute every contract call themselves, and all of them must end up with the same state.

<ask type="predict">A lottery contract needs a random winner, so its author proposes that the contract calls the operating system's random number generator. What happens when the nodes each execute that call?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
Works fine. Each node runs it and gets a winner, then they vote on which result to keep and the majority wins. That's literally what consensus is for.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="wrong" score="0.1">"they vote on which result to keep" is not something the nodes can do here: each node draws its own number, so each gets a different winner, and there is no result that most of them share. Consensus settles which transactions run and in what order; every node then computes the outcome itself and expects to get the same one.</grade>

Put numbers on it rather than take that on trust. Say 1,000 nodes execute the call and the lottery has 50 tickets, each equally likely to come out of a node's own generator.

<card>Can nodes vote on the result of a contract call that used local randomness? :: No. Each node would compute a different result, so no majority forms. Consensus fixes the order of transactions; execution must then give every node the same state.</card>

<note>Consensus agrees on which transactions run and in what order, not on results. Every node recomputes the results itself.</note>

<ask type="predict">Of those 1,000 nodes, about how many would you expect to name any one particular ticket as the winner, and is that anywhere near a majority?</ask>
@@ verify
# 1,000 nodes, 50 equally likely tickets: expected count naming one given ticket
assert 1000 / 50 == 20 and 20 < 1000 / 2
import random
random.seed(7)
draws = [random.randrange(50) for _ in range(1000)]
top = max(draws.count(t) for t in range(50))
assert top < 500                      # no ticket gets anywhere near a majority
assert '20' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ why-first-seen-rule-idk
situation: why
topic: The longest-chain rule and forks
options: {"why":true}
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"must":["first"],"forbid":["grade"],"askTypes":["predict","short","explain"],"askMustNot":["never (agree|converge)","permanent","split","X keeps A"],"mustNot":["most work","longest chain wins"]}
