@@ tutor
**Question 1 of 3**

<ask type="short">What does a wallet store: coins, or something else? Say what it stores and where the coins are.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
it stores the private keys. the coins arent in it, theyre just entries on the chain that those keys are allowed to spend
@@ tutor
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"it stores the private keys" is right, and so is the second half: the coins are records on the chain, and the keys are what authorise spending them.</grade>

**Question 2 of 3**

<ask type="explain">A hierarchical deterministic wallet shows you a seed phrase once, then generates a fresh address for each payment you receive. Months later you enter the phrase on a new device. Which of your addresses come back, and why?</ask>
@@ learner answer unsure
only the first adress i think. the others were made later so they cant be in the phrase, youd need a new backup each time
@@ tutor
<grade verdict="wrong" score="0.1">"the others were made later so they cant be in the phrase" assumes each address is new random data. In a hierarchical deterministic wallet every key is computed from the seed: the same seed and the same derivation path give the same sequence of keys, so all of the addresses come back and one backup covers them.</grade>

<card>Why does one seed phrase restore every address of a hierarchical deterministic wallet? :: Each key is derived deterministically from the seed along a derivation path. Same seed and same path give the same keys, in the same order, on any device.</card>

**Question 3 of 3**

<ask type="short">Two wallet apps are given the same seed phrase. One shows your funds and the other shows a zero balance. Assuming neither has a bug, what is the most likely reason?</ask>
@@ learner answer unsure
diffrent derivation path maybe? same seed but the 2nd app derives other keys from it, so its looking at adresses i never used
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"same seed but the 2nd app derives other keys from it" is the reason: the seed fixes the whole tree of keys, and the derivation path decides which branch of it an app looks at.</grade>

<summary>**Score:** 2 of 3 correct.

**Strengths:** what a wallet holds (keys, not coins), and the role of the derivation path, which you worked out in the last question from the idea you had just been corrected on.

**Misconception to fix:** that each new address needs its own backup. Every key is derived from the seed, so the phrase restores all of them, on any device.

**Review:** deterministic derivation from a seed, and what a seed phrase backup covers: all keys on every path, provided the restoring app uses the same derivation path.</summary>
